From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject:  Re: U. of Washington AUP
Message-ID: <1993Apr1.192757.14927@eff.org>
Date: Thu, 1 Apr 1993 19:27:57 GMT

This is a quick critique of the U. of Washington AUP.

> o No one will obtain unauthorized access to other users'
>   accounts and files.

How does authorization occur? (I think dorm search policies
make good models)

> If abuse of computer systems
> occurs, those responsible for such abuse will be held
> legally accountable.

Might be good to mention discipinary action will take place
through regular university channels.

> o The intended use of all computer accounts, for University
>   research, instruction, and administrative purposes, must be
>   respected.

So, sending email to a friend or reading rec.fishing is illegal?
The policy doesn't even use your word "education", it says
"instruction". I think a switch to "education" would be good.

I would think that students (at least) should be able to use the
library, the gyms, some classrooms (after hours), and the computer for
personal (noncommerial) and recreational reasons. Such use would of
course have lower priority that other uses.

Does the state really say that students can only use the library and
gyms for instructional and research purposes? It it does, this
(strange) rule should be quoted and referenced in the policy so that
the policy will be as restrictive as necessary, but not more so.

- Carl
